WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
My book How Not To Age,

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.000
is all about the mechanisms of aging

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.248
and how we can affect them

00:00:05.248 --> 00:00:06.884
through diet and lifestyle.

00:00:06.884 --> 00:00:08.755
Can looking at epigenetics,

00:00:08.755 --> 00:00:10.695
modifiers of gene expression

00:00:10.695 --> 00:00:14.020
help predict
our health span and lifespan?

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.408
Watch to find out.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:22.465
"Epigenetic Clocks
for Testing Your Biological Age"

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.920
Epigenetics-the differential
expression of genes-

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.740
both establishes the character
and function of a cell

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.320
and maintains that identity
over time

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.660
through round after round
of cell division.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.180
So, a heart cell
stays a heart cell

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.280
and divides
to make more heart cells,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.080
instead of skin cells
or kidney cells,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.480
even though all our main cells

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.540
have the same
entire complement of DNA

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:46.620
to potentially be anything.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.800
This is accomplished
by methylation,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.040
chemical markers that silence
inappropriate genes

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:53.820
in a particular cell.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.640
The fidelity
of that maintenance

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.420
of methylation is good-
97 percent to 99.9 percent

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.760
every division-but not perfect.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.660
Over time, those tiny errors
may add up

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.480
and may help explain why
the methylation patterns

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.480
of identical twins
drift apart as they age.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.500
The epigenetic markers
of young identical twins

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.000
are essentially
indistinguishable

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.800
but then diverge over time.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.820
Identical twins have
the same DNA, the same genes,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.460
but the difference
in gene expression

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.800
among older
identical twin pairs

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.620
were found to be
about four times greater

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.020
than those observed
in young pairs.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.560
This may result in them each
getting different diseases.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.320
An age-related disease
like Alzheimer's

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.260
only has an identical twin
concordance rate

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.320
of about 50 percent,
meaning if one twin gets it,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.540
there's only
about a coin flip chance

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.780
that the other will too,
despite identical DNA.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.480
Or, even if they do
both get it,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.180
the disease may manifest
decades apart.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.080
Any epigenetic differences
that may contribute

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.520
to differential disease rates

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.920
may arise from having
different diets and lifestyles

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.940
or may be a result
of random epigenetic drift.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.460
However, there are
certain DNA sites

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.100
on our chromosomes
that predictably methylate

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.960
or demethylate as we age.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.140
So predictable, in fact,
it's like clockwork.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.260
One of the earliest attempts
to study aging

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:23.400
and the epigenome

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.800
(which is only
like a dozen years ago)

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.980
found that the DNA
from a 103-year-old

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.680
appeared to be
less methylated overall

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.720
than the DNA
of a newborn infant,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.120
suggesting, perhaps,
that aging involves

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.640
the general loss
of epigenetic markings.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.700
We now know
it's more complicated than that.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.060
Of the methylation sites
that reliably change as we age,

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.180
about 60 percent go
from methylated to unmethylated,

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.480
and the other 40 percent become
more methylated over time.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.120
Some so reliably change
with age

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.140
that they've been considered

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.500
a "molecular crystal ball
for human aging."

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.320
In a remarkable triumph
of Big Data,

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.600
out of the millions
of methylation sites

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:04.380
in our DNA,

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.220
a tiny subset
so dependably shift over time

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.140
that you can predict
someone's age

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:10.980
within a few years

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.580
just by strategically measuring
the methylation pattern

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.640
in a few hundred
or even just a few dozen sites

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.100
in someone's
three-billion-letter genome.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.380
Over just the last few years,

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:25.740
these "epigenetic clocks"
have become established

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.140
as robust measures
of chronological age,

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.920
surpassing telomere length
as the best age predictor.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:34.440
Who cares, though?

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.860
I mean, why invent some costly
Rube Goldberg approach

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.860
to divining someone's age
when you can simply ask them?

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.400
Well, you can imagine
forensic applications,

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.340
the determination
of an unidentified victim's age

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:48.480
with a blood sample,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.060
but that's just scratching
the surface.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.780
The kicker is
that epigenetic clocks

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.240
don't just track
your chronological age

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.420
but appear to measure
your true biological age.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.400
In other words,
your epigenetic age

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.220
can better predict
your remaining life expectancy

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:06.780
than your calendar age.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.120
It's like science fiction.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.240
Feed a drop of blood
into some futuristic machine

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.460
that scans the placement
of chemical markers

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.280
on a strand of DNA,
and it spits out your true age,

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.100
reflecting a lifetime
of lifestyle choices.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.200
If the machine calculated

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.180
that you have
the DNA methylation pattern

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:27.600
of a 60-year-old, but you've
only had 50 birthdays,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.380
that would be an example of
"epigenetic age acceleration,"

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.960
when your epigenetic clock age
is older

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.700
than your actual
chronological age.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:39.120
That would be an indication
that you're aging too fast.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.100
As a 50-year-old,

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.340
you'd think you have another,
mm, 30 years on this Earth,

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.440
but because
the epigenetic clock shows

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.420
that you're aging
at such an accelerated pace,

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:51.120
it's more like you only have
another 20 years left.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:55.200
Every five years
of epigenetic age acceleration

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.040
is associated

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.460
with an 8 to 15 percent
increased risk of mortality.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.940
In addition to predicting
time-to-death,

00:05:03.715 --> 00:05:06.060
epigenetic clocks
also appear to foretell

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:08.700
healthspan indicators,
such as cognitive decline,

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:12.840
frailty, arthritis,
and the progression of diseases

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.180
like Alzheimer's
and Parkinson's.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:16.200
As you can imagine,

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.660
the insurance agency*
has jumped on this,

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.580
and your premiums
may soon be determined

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:21.960
by your epigenetic age.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.620
But it's not some
Gypsy fortune teller curse

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:26.400
set in stone.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.980
You can change the rate
at which you age

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:31.620
and may soon be able
to use epigenetic clocks

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:32.760
to track your progress,

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.220
potentially presenting
a radically faster

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:38.869
and cheaper way to test
anti-aging interventions.

